{"id":"aaa9b5fb-c390-42d1-bfba-d6851346b762","object":"chat.completion","created":1785002546,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError('case must be a dict')\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if nodes is None or required_symbols is None:\\n        raise ValueError('missing nodes or required_symbols')\\n    node_map = {}\\n    parent_children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node.get('id')\\n        if nid is None:\\n            raise ValueError('node missing id')\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        pid = node.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            parent_children.setdefault(pid, []).append(nid)\\n    required_set = set(required_symbols)\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    # sort by depth descending, then id for stable tie break\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node.get('removable', False):\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_set:\\n            continue\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        children = parent_children.get(nid, [])\\n        # check if any child is retained\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children):\\n            continue\\n        # all conditions met, remove this node\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the task. It's SE-PE-01. The output schema: JSON object with \"removed_node_ids\" (array of strings) and \"retained_node_ids\" (array of strings). The solve function takes a case payload as described. The payload has \"nodes\" list and \"required_symbols\" list.\n\nThe algorithm: grammar-guided node reduction similar to Perses syntax-guided reduction. We have a tree with nodes having id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Need to remove nodes while ensuring required symbols remain present and other constraints. The description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to produce the list of removed node IDs in order of removal, and list of retained node IDs (presumably all nodes not removed, including root and required symbols).\n\nWe need to interpret the algorithm: For each node, we consider removal if removable flag is True? The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a node can be removed only if it is not root, not having a symbol in required_symbols? Actually \"required symbol\" likely means any node whose symbol is in required_symbols list. And \"node with retained children\" means if after removal, some children would be retained? Actually the phrase: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That is a pre-condition for removal. Also \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that are not removed, we cannot remove it, because that would leave children without parent? Or maybe removing a node would delete its subtree? Typically in Perses, removing a node means replacing it with its children? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". But the output only has removed_node_ids. Possibly it's a simple removal where you delete a node entirely, but if it has children, they would be orphaned? The constraint \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" suggests that if a node has children that are not removed, you cannot remove the parent, because that would leave children. That implies we can only remove leaf nodes or nodes whose entire subtree is also removed? Actually \"retained children\" means children that are retained in the final output. So before deciding removal, we need to know which nodes will be retained. This is a typical reduction algorithm that works bottom-up: decide removal recursively. The \"consider deeper nodes first\" suggests we process nodes in decreasing depth order. For each node, we decide if it can be removed. When we remove a node, we remove it and its entire subtree (since children are descendant). But the constraint \"node with retained children\" would then prohibit removal if any child is retained. So to remove a node, all its descendants must also be removed (or be candidates for removal). But we are deciding bottom-up: we first decide on deepest nodes, then move up. For a leaf node, if removable, we can remove it as long as required symbols remain represented (the required symbols count in the tree after removal would still have all required symbols? Must check). Then for a parent, we can remove it only if it is removable, and all its children have been removed (i.e., none are retained), and required symbols still present after removing the whole subtree. That matches typical reduction.\n\nAlternative: The phrase \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" explicitly forbids removing a node that has any child that is retained. So we must ensure children are removed first if we want to remove parent. That's bottom-up.\n\nThus algorithm:\n1. Build adjacency: parent -> children.\n2. Collect all nodes by id.\n3. Determine order: nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id (stable tie break). (id is string, but we can compare lexicographically as given; but example IDs like \"n1\", \"n2\" so string compare works.)\n4. Maintain set of retained_node_ids initially all node ids.\n5. Process nodes in that order:\n   - For each node, check conditions for removal:\n     a) node is removable == True (from node dict).\n     b) node id is not root? Actually root has removable=false, so fine.\n     c) node's symbol is NOT in required_symbols list (if symbol in required_symbols, can't remove).\n     d) All children of this node are not retained? i.e., for each child id, check if child id is not in retained_node_ids. If any child is retained, skip removal.\n     e) After hypothetical removal of this node (and implicitly its subtree because children would be removed if they are not retained? Wait, children may have been removed already in processing, so retained condition ensures no children retained. So removal means removing this node from retained set.\n     f) Check \"all required symbols remain represented\" after removal. That is: before removal, the retained set includes nodes covering all required symbols. After removal, we need to ensure that there is still at least one node for each required symbol in retained set (since we might remove the only node of a required symbol). So we need to check if for any required_symbol s, the only retained node(s) with that symbol are the node being removed. Because node's symbol could be required. If node's symbol is required, we cannot remove it (already condition c). But what if node's symbol is not required, but its removal might cause a required symbol to disappear because that required symbol existed only in its subtree? But since children are removed before parent, and children have been processed, any required symbol that was only present in node's descendants would have been removed already (if we removed those descendants). But we need to ensure that when we remove a node, we are not removing the last representation of a required symbol that is present in the node itself (but that's condition c) or present in some descendant that we have already removed? Actually we remove node's subtree only if we remove node. But if we removed descendant nodes that had required symbols, that would violate condition for those descendant nodes. So condition c at descendant level would have prevented removal if symbol required. So if a required symbol is only present in a node that is not removed, and we remove its ancestor, the required symbol remains because the descendant is still retained (since we haven't removed it). But if we remove the ancestor and we have not removed the descendant, then we violate \"node with retained children\" because descendant is retained. So condition d prevents that. So condition d ensures no retained children under a removed node. Therefore, the only way a required symbol could be lost is if the node being removed itself has a required symbol (already prevented) or if the required symbol is present only in descendants that have been removed (impossible because descendants are removed only if they were removable and not required). However, suppose a required symbol appears only in a node that is removable and not required? Actually it is required, so cannot be removed. So condition c protects. So actually condition f may be redundant if we enforce conditions c and d properly. But the description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" We can implement by checking after hypothetical removal: check that for each required symbol, there exists at least one node in retained (after removal) with that symbol. Since we are only removing one node (and its subtree is already removed), we can just check whether the node being removed is the only node with its symbol? But not exactly: consider required symbol \"s1\", there could be multiple nodes with s1. Removing one might be fine. The condition is that all required symbols remain represented; that is, for each symbol in required_symbols, the count of nodes with that symbol in retained set after removal is >=1. So we need to compute pre-removal counts, and decrement for the node's symbol. If any count becomes 0, removal not allowed.\n6. If all conditions pass, remove the node: remove its id from retained_node_ids. Record its id in removed_node_ids list (in the order of removal).\n7. After processing all nodes, the retained_node_ids set contains the final retained nodes. Sort it (probably by id? The output says \"sorted retained IDs\". So we need to sort retained_node_ids alphabetically? They didn't specify sort key. But likely alphabetical order of strings. The example outputs? Not given. But we can sort them as strings.\n8. removed_node_ids list should preserve the order of removal (the order in which we processed and removed nodes). That will be descending depth order with tie break on id.\n\nImportant: The initial set of retained_node_ids includes all nodes. We only remove nodes as we process. Process order: sort by depth descending, then by id (string). For tie break, use id string comparison. So we can sort: nodes.sort(key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id'])).\n\nEdge cases: Root is depth 0, removable false, so won't be removed. Required nodes: they have removable maybe false or true? In examples, nodes with required symbol \"s1\" have removable false for level 1 nodes? In first fixture: n1 has symbol s1, removable false; n4 also has symbol s1 but is under n2 and removable true. So n4 has a required symbol but is removable. So we must not remove nodes that have required symbol, even if removable true. Condition c.\n\nAlso, note that nodes that are not removable cannot be removed regardless.\n\nCheck: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" That's for ordering.\n\nImplementation steps in solve(case):\n- Validate payload: has 'nodes' list, each with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. required_symbols is list. Expect all present, otherwise raise ValueError.\n- Build mapping: id->node dict. Also parent->children adjacency.\n- Ensure root exists (parent null).\n- Precompute required_symbol_set.\n- Initialize retained = set(all node ids).\n- Create removed_order list.\n- Create sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n- For each node in sorted_nodes:\n    - if node['id'] not in retained: continue (already removed? But we are processing original list, and we might have removed children already, but parent may still be in retained. That's fine.)\n    - Check removability: if not node['removable']: continue\n    - Check root: if node['parent'] is None: continue (though removable false so already caught)\n    - Check symbol: if node['symbol'] in required_symbol_set: continue\n    - Check children: if any child_id of node that is in retained: continue. Because if any child retained, can't remove.\n    - Check required symbols representation: compute counts of each required symbol in retained set. Simpler: for each required symbol, we need to know if there will still be at least one node with that symbol after removal. Since the node's symbol is not required (already ensured), removing this node does not remove any required symbol. But what if there is only one node with a required symbol that is a descendant that is still retained? That cannot happen because descendant is retained and not removed yet. But if the node's child retained has a required symbol, then condition d says children retained, so removal not allowed. So we don't even get to required check. If no children retained, then any descendant nodes are not retained (since children are not retained, and we have been processing bottom-up, so all descendants would have been already processed and either removed or retained. But if a descendant is retained, its parent must be retained (because parent removal requires all children removed). So if a node has no retained children, then all its descendants are either removed or were never retained. So there is no descendant with a required symbol retained. Thus required symbols must be present elsewhere. So condition f might be redundant but we can still implement for safety. But we also need to consider that the node might have multiple children, some removed, some never existed? Actually every child has to be processed. If a child is retained, parent cannot be removed. So the scenario where a child is removed but not the parent is normal. If all children are removed, parent can be removed if other conditions hold. What about required symbols that appear only in the node itself? Already excluded. So after passing children condition, the only possible loss of required symbols is if the node's own symbol is required (excluded) or if the required symbol only existed in nodes that are already removed (which would have been prevented at their removal). So it's safe. But we can still do a check: before removal, we can compute counts of required symbols in retained set. Then after removal, decrement count of node's symbol. If any count goes to zero, skip. Since node's symbol is not required, counts unchanged. So we can skip this check. However, to be safe and follow the instruction, we can implement it.\n\nSimplify: no need for counts because node's symbol is not required. But if there is a required symbol that is the same as node's symbol, we already skipped. So no issue.\n\nThus implementation: for each node in sorted_nodes:\n    if node['id'] not in retained: continue\n    if not node['removable']: continue\n    if node['symbol'] in required_symbol_set: continue\n    if node['parent'] is None: continue (though removable false)\n    children = adjacency.get(node['id'], [])\n    if any(child_id in retained for child_id in children): continue\n    # No children retained, safe to remove\n    retained.remove(node['id'])\n    removed_order.append(node['id'])\n\nAfter loop, retained set contains final retained IDs. Convert to list, sort (using default string sort). removed_order list as is.\n\nReturn {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(retained)}.\n\nBut note: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids sorted (presumably ascending). removed_node_ids in removal order.\n\nCheck that this matches Perses: It removes nodes bottom-up, only if entire subtree can be removed.\n\nNow, we need to implement as Python source string inside JSON response.\n\nWe must be careful about JSON escaping.\n\nWe'll produce a response: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ... return ...\"}\n\nMake sure to handle errors: raise ValueError if required fields missing.\n\nWe'll include validation.\n\nEdge: payload could have _case_nonce etc.\n\nNow, write code.\n\nWe'll produce final JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":3503,"total_tokens":5672,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3087},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}